## 4. Record — ticket 003 in full

`espalier/maps/2026-08-07-maintenance-model/tickets/003-scan-record.md`:

```markdown
---
ticket: 003
title: How a completed scan is recorded for the team
type: grilling
status: closed
blocked_by: [002]
claimed_by: junhanliu.dev@gmail.com
claimed_at: 2026-08-07T10:01:00Z
---

## Question

Teammates currently can't see that this week's scan happened, so the
reminder nags everyone anyway. Where does the "a scan ran, and was the
tree clean" fact live? The team's first instinct: have the post-merge
hook append a row to a tracked `scan-log.tsv` so the record propagates
to every clone automatically.

## Resolution

The team-wide scan record is a one-line tracked stamp written by the
deliberate scan step itself, committed as its own commit by the week's
rotating owner. The post-merge-hook instinct is rejected: it collides
with `rules/automation-boundaries.md#tracked-writes` — no hook or
background automation ever writes a tracked file; tracked files change
only through a deliberate, human-gated step that commits its own work.
Hooks stay read-only on tracked files.

Relationship to the existing per-clone stamp
(`wiki/dev-tooling.md#scan-stamps`): keep both, distinct facts. The
tracked stamp carries the team-wide fact — this week's scan ran and the
tree was clean. The gitignored `tooling/state/.docscan-last-run` stays
the per-clone fact that reminder logic reads. A scan that ends with
unresolved findings satisfies only the clone that ran it: it updates the
per-clone stamp but does not produce the team-wide clean stamp, so the
team record never silently duplicates or overstates the per-clone one.

Citations:
- `rules/automation-boundaries.md#tracked-writes` — rule collision,
  resolved: follow the rule; scan step commits its own stamp, hooks
  never write tracked files.
- `wiki/dev-tooling.md#scan-stamps` — wiki duplication, resolved: keep
  both stamps with defined roles (team fact vs per-clone fact); do not
  replace or re-implement the documented per-clone stamp.

Open question (default — revisit): exact path and line format of the
tracked stamp. Conservative default: a single tracked one-line file
(e.g. `tooling/scan-stamp`) overwritten by each weekly scan commit,
carrying date, owner, and clean status. Human was unavailable for this
naming detail; it does not change the decision above.
```

